lesson chemistry-solid-liquid-and-gas v=2 format=teach grade=2 unit=2 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Solid, liquid and gas"
  goal "Tell a solid, a liquid and a gas apart by how their particles are arranged and how they move, and use that to say what each will do."

  look {
    scene 35x13 {
      row three at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        particles s spread=0 count=16 moving=1
        particles l spread=1 count=16 moving=1
        particles g spread=2 count=16 moving=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Everything is made of particles too small to see. In a solid they are held in rows and
      jiggle on the spot. In a liquid they touch but slide past each other. In a gas they are far
      apart and fly everywhere.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked chem.which-state s=1 m=0 level=medium
    }
    practice chem.which-state count=3 hard-count=2 seed=5
    level hard {
      practice chem.state-why count=1 seed=7
    }
    practice chem.find-the-gas count=2 seed=2
    practice chem.jar-like count=1 seed=5
    practice chem.state-clue count=2 seed=2 hard-seed=7
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.state-why s=1 easy-s=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.still-there n=12 f=4 easy-f=3 hard-n=24
  }
  remember {
    say "Held in rows is a solid, touching but sliding is a liquid, far apart is a gas."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The first two questions read a state off one picture, which is the way in. The jars in a
    different order and the question of what the stuff will do move the unknown from the state to the thing
    and to its behaviour. The two-star question asks for the reason a state behaves as it does, in
    terms of the particles. The three-star question never says how many particles there are: the
    child counts them, has to know that heating makes no new particles and loses none, and then
    works out a share. Children holding hands in a line, then shuffling, then running round the
    garden is the demonstration; the jars are a record of it, not a replacement.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the jars show the moving marks, a liquid without them is named once in the teacher's pen, the two jars to choose between hold a solid and a gas, the things to place are a stone and the air in a balloon, the two-star asks why a solid keeps its shape, and the three-star jar has twelve particles with one in three getting out."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the jars show no moving marks, the two jars to choose between hold a liquid beside a solid or a gas and the liquid is asked for, the things to place are an ice cube and steam, the two-star asks why a liquid takes the shape of its cup and then why a gas fills the jar, and the three-star jar has twenty-four particles with one in four getting out."
  }
}
